  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `src/execution_coverage/progress.rs#mod[tests]/impl[RecordingTerm/indicatif::TermLike]/fn[move_cursor_left]` meets the built-in's criteria.\n\n- **Form:** It is a fenced citation with commentary, placed on an explicit function with a body (`Ok(())`). That is the form the built-in allows for a function-level exception.\n- **Substantive reason:** The commentary gives a real reason. `indicatif::TermLike` requires the callback. `RecordingTerm` is a unit-test-only adapter, because it sits in `#[cfg(test)] mod tests`. The no-op has no application behavior to test.\n- **Agreement with the source:** The tests in `mod[tests]` are `stage_and_conversion_counts_are_visible_then_cleared`, `preparation_activity_keeps_updating_without_a_stage_change` and `terminal_policy_and_hidden_progress_emit_no_display`. They are display tests that run `Progress` against the recording adapter. I found nothing in them or in `Progress` that requests horizontal cursor movement. I did not read indicatif's own source, so I did not check that its drawing never calls `move_cursor_left`. The rest of the claim is consistent with the file.\n- **Real-terminal claim:** The claim that real terminal movement is exercised through a PTY integration test holds. `tests/execution_coverage.rs#fn[coverage_progress_on_a_pty_preserves_reports_and_plain_output_policy]` opens a real PTY with `openpty` and runs the coverage progress path.\n- **No redundancy:** The item has no {impl} or {test} reference for the same target. The citation is not redundant.",
